#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:109
msgid "Django checks"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:110
msgid "Looks for configuration problems without touching anything."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:112
msgid ""
"Runs the framework checks: models, admin, URLs, templates and security "
"settings. It is what should come out clean before any deploy."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:116
msgid "After changing settings.py, to confirm nothing broke."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:121
msgid "Deploy checks"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:124
msgid ""
"Adds the production-only checks: HTTPS, cookies, HSTS. Expect warnings in "
"development."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:133
msgid "Media storage"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:135
msgid ""
"Checks that MEDIA_ROOT points where the files really are and that the "
"sensitive folders are not served by the web server."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:139
msgid ""
"FileField stores paths relative to MEDIA_ROOT. If the variable points "
"somewhere else, nothing is lost but Django stops finding the files: every "
"download 404s and new uploads land in the wrong place. It fails silently, "
"which is why this exists. It also checks the .htaccess files that stop the "
"web server from handing out certificates and identity documents on its own."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:146
msgid "After moving the media folder, or after a deploy."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:151
msgid "Files per field"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:154
msgid "How many files to check per model field."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:158
msgid "Search under"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:161
msgid ""
"If a file is missing, look for it by name under this path and say where it "
"is."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:168
msgid "Check over HTTP"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:171
msgid ""
"Actually request a sensitive file from outside and confirm the web server "
"answers 404."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:180
msgid "Certified documents health"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:182
msgid ""
"Checks that the three files of every certified document are on disk and that "
"their fingerprints still match."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:186
msgid ""
"Two different failures: a record pointing at a deleted file, and a file "
"whose stored hash no longer matches what is on disk. The second one is the "
"serious one — it means the certification is lying."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:191
msgid "Monthly, and after any migration of the media folder."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:197
msgid "Anti-scan trap"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:199
msgid "Checks that no legitimate route collides with the scanner trap."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:202
msgid ""
"The trap catches any path containing one of COMMON_ATTACK_TERMS and blocks "
"the IP. A badly chosen term hijacks a real route and locks out real users. "
"This reports collisions respecting the URLconf order, which is what actually "
"decides it."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:207
msgid "Every time COMMON_ATTACK_TERMS changes."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:213
msgid "Migration state"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:214
msgid "Lists which migrations are applied and which are pending."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:216
msgid ""
"Nothing runs. It is the way to see whether the database matches the code "
"before deciding to migrate."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:219
msgid "Right after deploying, before running migrate."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:224
msgid "Show plan"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:226
msgid "Lists them in the order they would be applied."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:237
msgid "Apply migrations"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:238
msgid "Brings the database schema up to date with the code."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:240
msgid ""
"Applies every pending migration. Run the migration state check first to see "
"what is going to happen."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:243
msgid "After deploying code that adds fields or constraints."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:248
#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:274
msgid "No questions"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:251
#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:277
msgid "Required here: there is nobody to answer a prompt."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:256
msgid ""
"A migration can lock tables. On a busy database, run it out of office hours."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:262
msgid "Collect static files"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:263
msgid "Publishes the CSS and JS so the browser gets them."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:265
msgid ""
"In production static files are served with a hash in the name, so a change "
"is invisible until this runs. If a page looks broken after a deploy, this is "
"usually why."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:269
msgid "After every deploy that touches CSS or JS."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:281
msgid "Clear first"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:284
msgid ""
"Deletes what was collected before. Slower, but leaves no orphan files behind."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:293
msgid "Compile translations"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:294
msgid "Turns the .po catalogs into the .mo files Django reads."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:296
msgid ""
"Editing a translation changes the .po file, but Django only reads the "
"compiled .mo. Until this runs, the new text shows in English."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:300
msgid "After translating with Rosetta or editing a .po by hand."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:305
msgid "Language"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:309
msgid "Which catalog to compile."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:316
msgid "Clear the cache"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:317
msgid "Empties the application cache."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:319
msgid ""
"Useful when something stale is being served. Note that it also clears the "
"public OTP rate-limit counters, which live in cache: right after running "
"this, those limits start from zero."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:323
msgid "After changing data that a page keeps cached."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:327
msgid ""
"It resets the public OTP rate limits. Do not run it while someone is "
"hammering the verification page."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:333
msgid "Daily GEA code"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:334
msgid "Issues the daily code and mails it to its recipients."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:336
msgid ""
"The same thing the scheduled job does. Running it by hand sends real email, "
"so only use it if the scheduled run failed."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:339
msgid "The cron did not run and the code for today is missing."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:342
msgid "This sends real email to the configured recipients."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:350
msgid "Database backup"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:351
msgid "Writes a full dump of the database to disk."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:353
msgid ""
"Produces a file with everything in the database. Where it lands matters: "
"anyone who can read that path can read every record, including the encrypted "
"personal data fields."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:357
msgid "Right before applying a risky migration."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:362
msgid "Output folder"
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:365
msgid "Folder for the dump, relative to the project root."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:371
msgid ""
"The dump contains every record. Keep it out of any folder the web server can "
"serve."
msgstr ""

